 The cross-sectional factor literature is large and its gross numbers are spectacular, but almost none of it survives this account's constraints. Liu, Tsyvinski and Wu found weekly long-short spreads of **2.5–4.1% for 1–4-week momentum and −3.4% for size** on 2014–2018 data. Their own caveat is that the results ignore "trading costs and the feasibility of short selling." When BTC replaces the short leg, most momentum strategies lose significance ([NBER w25882](https://www.nber.org/papers/w25882.pdf)). Later constraint-focused work removes most of what remained. Size and volume anomalies **"originate from micro-cap coins of negligible economic importance,"** momentum alpha comes "largely from short positions," and anomaly returns cluster in bull markets and weaken over time ([Fieberg, Liedtke & Zaremba, IRFA 2024](https://open.icm.edu.pl/handle/123456789/25544)). In the top-30 coins, momentum was **negative and insignificant from August 2020 to 2023**. Value-weighted across ~2,500 coins it was significantly negative at −3.40% a week, and a single coin's jump produced a −255% week ([Äijö et al. 2025](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11408-025-00474-9)). Correcting for survivorship removes any positive link between returns and one-week momentum. That bias runs at **62% a year for equal-weighted portfolios versus 0.93% for value-weighted ones** ([Ammann et al. 2022](https://alexandria.unisg.ch/handle/20.500.14171/108037)).
 
