 Validation has to guard against the main way a solo researcher fools himself: testing many variants and keeping the best. Harvey, Liu and Zhu argue that a new factor needs a **t-ratio above 3.0, not 2.0**, and that most claimed findings are likely false ([NBER w20592](https://nber.org/papers/w20592)). The Deflated Sharpe Ratio adjusts a backtest Sharpe for the number of trials and for fat tails ([Bailey & López de Prado](https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=2460551)). The Probability of Backtest Overfitting estimates, via combinatorial cross-validation, how often the in-sample winner underperforms out of sample ([Bailey et al.](https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=2326253)), and an R implementation exists ([CRAN pbo](https://ftp.fau.de/cran/web/packages/pbo/vignettes/pbo.html)). In practice that means writing down each candidate addition and its parameter grid before running it, and logging every variant tried, failures included, so DSR and PBO use the true trial count. Refits should be walk-forward. The post-ETF period from January 2024 to the present should be a final holdout opened once: volume has shifted toward US weekday hours since the ETFs ([The Block citing Kaiko](https://www.theblock.co/news/ecosystems/2024-06-30-bitcoin-sees-all-time-low-weekend-trading-levels-following-etf-approvals-kaiko-302705)), and that is the regime the strategy will trade in. Fees should be modelled at both 0.25/0.50 and the reported 0.50/0.90 US rate, with limit orders given partial fills, no-fills and post-fill drift. The benchmarks are BTC buy-and-hold and the fixed BTC/ETH mix, after costs and short-term tax. Most important, any new ingredient must improve on **the existing 28-day vol-scaled candidate**, not on BTC. The incumbent already captures the trend premium, so an overlay that merely re-derives it will look good against BTC and add nothing.
 
